Scenario: replicated counter audit / read write overlaps.

A single atomic register x has the listed finite values and initially holds initial_facts.x. A read returns the current value and leaves it unchanged. A write sets x to argument and returns done. CAS(expected,replacement) returns yes and changes x to replacement exactly when x equals expected; otherwise it returns no and leaves x unchanged.
Every recorded operation is complete and must occur exactly once in a one-at-a-time ordering that reproduces every recorded reply. If return(i) < invoke(j), i must be earlier than j. Equality of those times creates no real-time precedence. Overlapping intervals impose no order by themselves. Row order and operation names impose no order. A feasible ordering must satisfy both the state-transition semantics and all strict real-time precedences.
In an option, before refers to this atomic ordering, not invocation order. The two relations must hold together in at least one feasible ordering of ALL operations, not necessarily in every ordering. All possible permutations satisfying the stated rules are eligible, including permutations not displayed. The primitive semantics and constraints in the data spell out these same rules.

Exhaustive primitive data (JSON; array order supplies table row/column order):
```json
{
  "family_id": "F30",
  "atomic_model": {
    "domains": {
      "register_values": [
        1,
        5,
        3,
        8
      ],
      "operation_ids": [
        "A",
        "B",
        "C",
        "D",
        "E",
        "F"
      ],
      "order_positions": [
        1,
        2,
        3,
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "reply_values": [
        1,
        5,
        3,
        8,
        "yes",
        "no",
        "done"
      ]
    },
    "initial_facts": {
      "x": 1,
      "all_operations_complete": true
    },
    "semantics": {
      "read": {
        "reply_matches": "pre_value",
        "post_matches": "pre_value"
      },
      "write": {
        "reply": "done",
        "post_matches": "argument"
      },
      "cas": {
        "comparison": [
          "pre_value",
          "expected"
        ],
        "on_equal": {
          "reply": "yes",
          "post_matches": "replacement"
        },
        "on_unequal": {
          "reply": "no",
          "post_matches": "pre_value"
        }
      }
    },
    "operations": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "invoke": 0,
        "return": 15,
        "kind": "write",
        "argument": 8,
        "reply": "done"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "invoke": 10,
        "return": 25,
        "kind": "write",
        "argument": 1,
        "reply": "done"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "invoke": 20,
        "return": 38,
        "kind": "write",
        "argument": 8,
        "reply": "done"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "invoke": 17,
        "return": 28,
        "kind": "read",
        "reply": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "invoke": 16,
        "return": 28,
        "kind": "read",
        "reply": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "invoke": 1,
        "return": 15,
        "kind": "read",
        "reply": 8
      }
    ],
    "constraints": {
      "ordering": "a permutation of all operation_ids",
      "real_time_rule": {
        "condition": "return(i) < invoke(j)",
        "requirement": "i precedes j"
      },
      "state_consistency": "Adjacent operations share the preceding post_value as the following pre_value; the first pre_value is initial x.",
      "reply_consistency": "Every recorded reply must match the operation semantics."
    },
    "query": {
      "type": "existential_conjunction_choice",
      "criterion": "At least one feasible ordering satisfies both before_pairs of the selected alternative."
    }
  }
}
```

